{"type": "brief", "t": 1791221149.867, "patient": "SYN-X", "markdown": "# Pre-visit brief - Patient X (SYN-X)\n> SYNTHETIC RECORD - invented for a demo, not a real patient. For clinician review; not medical advice.\n\n## Snapshot\nPatient X is a male born in 1965 with type 2 diabetes diagnosed in 2014 and an upcoming routine diabetes follow-up in two weeks. [SQL:patient] [SQL:condition] The key changes are rapid kidney-function decline, new/worsening albuminuria, rising HbA1c, elevated blood pressure, sulfonylurea-associated hypoglycemia symptoms, and regular NSAID exposure. [SQL:lab_result] [SQL:vital_sign] [SQL:medication] [SYN-X-NOTE-0003] [SYN-X-NOTE-0004] [SYN-X-NOTE-0007] The visit should prioritize renal risk reassessment, medication safety, and completion of overdue kidney and retinal monitoring. [SQL:lab_result] [SQL:referral] [SYN-X-NOTE-0006]\n\n## What changed\n- **Kidney function.** eGFR fell from 68 mL/min/1.73m2 on 2025-03-04 to 59 on 2025-09-10, 52 on 2026-03-18, and 47 on 2026-08-26. [SQL:lab_result] The calculated eGFR slope is -14.17 mL/min/1.73m2 per year, which is a faster decline than the type 2 diabetes cohort p10 slope of -5.88 mL/min/1.73m2 per year because a more negative slope indicates faster decline. [SQL:lab_result] [SQL:cohort_benchmark]\n- **Albuminuria.** UACR rose from 22 mg/g on 2025-03-04 to 148 mg/g on 2026-03-18, indicating worsening albuminuria. [SQL:lab_result] No UACR was drawn with the most recent 2026-08-26 lab cycle, leaving current albuminuria status unknown after the eGFR decline continued. [SYN-X-NOTE-0006]\n- **Glycemic control.** HbA1c increased from 7.4% on 2025-03-04 to 7.9% on 2025-09-10, 8.3% on 2026-03-18, and 8.6% on 2026-08-26. [SQL:lab_result] The latest HbA1c of 8.6% is above the type 2 diabetes cohort p90 of 8.51%. [SQL:lab_result] [SQL:cohort_benchmark]\n- **Blood pressure.** Blood pressure rose from 138/84 mmHg on 2025-03-04 to 146/88 mmHg on 2026-03-18 and 149/90 mmHg on 2026-08-26 while lisinopril 20 mg daily remained active. [SQL:vital_sign] [SQL:medication] This matters because blood pressure control is a key modifiable factor for diabetic kidney disease progression. [MEDQUAD-02123#chunk-0]\n- **Medication-related symptoms and exposures.** The patient reported a warehouse job with long shifts and irregular meals, then had two late-afternoon shakiness/sweating episodes after skipped lunch with one home glucose of 62 mg/dL while taking glipizide as prescribed. [SYN-X-NOTE-0003] [SYN-X-NOTE-0004] He later asked about stopping the afternoon glipizide dose because of \u201clow sugar spells\u201d at work. [SYN-X-NOTE-0007] He also uses ibuprofen most days for knee pain and prefers it over acetaminophen. [SYN-X-NOTE-0003]\n\n## Medications to review\n| Medication | Concern | What the chart shows | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Metformin 1000 mg twice daily | renal dosing and sick-day safety as eGFR falls | Metformin 1000 mg twice daily has been active since 2014-05-01. [SQL:medication] eGFR has declined to 47 mL/min/1.73m2 with a rapid slope of -14.17 mL/min/1.73m2 per year, so continued dose appropriateness and monitoring frequency deserve review. [SQL:lab_result] | [SQL:medication] [SQL:lab_result] |\n| Glipizide 10 mg twice daily | hypoglycemia risk with skipped meals and declining kidney function | Glipizide 10 mg twice daily has been active since 2019-08-20. [SQL:medication] The patient had late-afternoon shakiness and sweating after skipped lunch, one home glucose was 62 mg/dL, and he asked about stopping the afternoon dose because of low sugar spells at work. [SYN-X-NOTE-0004] [SYN-X-NOTE-0007] | [SQL:medication] [SYN-X-NOTE-0004] [SYN-X-NOTE-0007] |\n| Lisinopril 20 mg once daily | renal-protective role but needs BP, creatinine/eGFR, and potassium monitoring | Lisinopril 20 mg daily has been active since 2016-02-11. [SQL:medication] Blood pressure is now 149/90 mmHg and eGFR has fallen to 47 mL/min/1.73m2, so adequacy of BP control and renal lab follow-up should be reassessed. [SQL:vital_sign] [SQL:lab_result] | [SQL:medication] [SQL:vital_sign] [SQL:lab_result] |\n| Ibuprofen 400 mg as needed, most days | potential nephrotoxic exposure during rapid eGFR decline | Ibuprofen 400 mg as needed, most days, has been active since 2025-09-10. [SQL:medication] The patient uses ibuprofen for knee pain and prefers it over acetaminophen while eGFR has fallen from 59 to 47 mL/min/1.73m2 since 2025-09-10. [SYN-X-NOTE-0003] [SQL:lab_result] | [SQL:medication] [SYN-X-NOTE-0003] [SQL:lab_result] |\n| Atorvastatin 40 mg once daily | cardiovascular risk optimization in diabetes with albuminuria | Atorvastatin 40 mg daily has been active since 2016-02-11. [SQL:medication] UACR is 148 mg/g and eGFR is 47 mL/min/1.73m2, both relevant to cardiovascular risk discussion in diabetes. [SQL:lab_result] | [SQL:medication] [SQL:lab_result] |\n\nThe highest-risk combination in the chart is declining kidney function plus ACE-inhibitor therapy plus frequent NSAID exposure, which warrants checking current renal labs and discussing non-NSAID pain strategies. [SQL:lab_result] [SQL:medication] [SYN-X-NOTE-0003] Glipizide use with irregular meals is temporally linked to symptomatic hypoglycemia and a documented home glucose of 62 mg/dL, even as overall HbA1c is rising. [SQL:lab_result] [SYN-X-NOTE-0003] [SYN-X-NOTE-0004]\n\n## Overdue monitoring and open care gaps\n- **Repeat urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio is overdue or missing from latest lab cycle.** UACR increased from 22 mg/g on 2025-03-04 to 148 mg/g on 2026-03-18. [SQL:lab_result] No UACR was drawn in the 2026-08-26 lab cycle despite continued eGFR decline. [SYN-X-NOTE-0006]\n- **Diabetic retinal screening remains overdue.** An ophthalmology referral for diabetic retinal screening opened on 2025-03-04 remains open. [SQL:referral] The patient missed the June 2025 appointment and reminder calls were not returned. [SYN-X-NOTE-0002] [SYN-X-NOTE-0005]\n- **Renal safety monitoring should be updated in light of rapid decline and medication risks.** The latest eGFR was 47 mL/min/1.73m2 on 2026-08-26 after a decline from 68 mL/min/1.73m2 on 2025-03-04. [SQL:lab_result] Active medications include lisinopril, metformin, glipizide, and most-days ibuprofen, making current renal function review clinically important. [SQL:medication]\n- **Foot exam timing should be confirmed.** The last documented annual foot exam was normal on 2025-03-04. [SYN-X-NOTE-0001] If no later exam exists, this is likely due for repeat around the current follow-up cycle. [SYN-X-NOTE-0001]\n\n## What the reference and research say\n### Clinical reference\n- The National Kidney Disease Education Program advises measuring the urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio at least once a year in all people with type 2 diabetes. [MEDQUAD-02122#chunk-1] It also advises calculating eGFR annually for all people with type 1 or type 2 diabetes. [MEDQUAD-02122#chunk-1] *Patient X has T2D, worsening UACR, and rapid eGFR decline, and the most recent lab cycle did not include UACR. [SQL:lab_result] [SYN-X-NOTE-0006]*\n- For diabetic kidney disease prevention, managing high blood pressure is described as essential to slowing progression. [MEDQUAD-02123#chunk-0] The reference notes that many people need multiple medications and that clinicians may prescribe a diuretic along with an ACE inhibitor or ARB. [MEDQUAD-02123#chunk-2] *Patient X\u2019s BP is 149/90 mmHg on lisinopril 20 mg daily, so BP goals, adherence, intensification, and kidney labs are central visit issues. [SQL:vital_sign] [SQL:medication]*\n- Hypoglycemia can occur when a person takes insulin or another medication that increases insulin levels and then skips a meal. [MEDQUAD-01393#chunk-0] Prevention guidance includes eating regular meals, having enough food at each meal, and trying not to skip meals or snacks. [MEDQUAD-01393#chunk-1] *Patient X takes glipizide, reports irregular meals and skipped lunch, and had symptomatic episodes with one home glucose of 62 mg/dL. [SQL:medication] [SYN-X-NOTE-0003] [SYN-X-NOTE-0004]*\n- The MEDQUAD search did not return specific citable guidance on metformin renal dose thresholds, sulfonylurea renal dose adjustment, NSAID avoidance in CKD, or retinal/foot exam intervals. [MEDQUAD-02122#chunk-1] *Those gaps should be handled by clinician judgment and local diabetes/CKD protocols rather than inferred from unavailable reference text. [MEDQUAD-02122#chunk-1]*\n\n### Research evidence\n- A study of 347 patients with stage 3 CKD found that macroalbuminuria, microalbuminuria, microscopic hematuria, and stage 3B CKD were independent predictors of renal dysfunction progression. [PMID-22545920] The same study reported higher adverse renal and cardiovascular outcome risks in stage 3B than stage 3A CKD. [PMID-22545920] *Patient X is stage G3a by eGFR 47 with UACR 148 mg/g and a rapid decline, so albuminuria and proximity to G3b raise concern. [SQL:lab_result]*\n- A coronary angiography cohort study defined albuminuria as urinary albumin-to-creatinine ratio of 30 \u03bcg/mg or greater and compared atherothrombotic event rates across albuminuria and prior MI groups. [PMID-25746373] Event rates were not significantly different between patients with albuminuria and no prior MI and patients with normoalbuminuria but prior MI, and risk was higher when both albuminuria and prior MI were present. [PMID-25746373] *Patient X\u2019s UACR of 148 mg/g supports treating albuminuria as a meaningful cardiovascular risk marker during diabetes follow-up. [SQL:lab_result]*\n- A study of 301 adults with diabetes found that CKD was associated with diabetic retinopathy only in the presence of albuminuria. [PMID-24636277] The authors interpreted albuminuria as important in linking CKD with diabetic retinopathy. [PMID-24636277] *Patient X has diabetes, CKD-range eGFR, albuminuria, and an overdue retinal referral, so eye screening is a high-priority gap. [SQL:lab_result] [SQL:referral]*\n- A randomized controlled trial abstract on ACE inhibitors in more severe CKD states that ACE inhibitors have shown protective effects in mild to moderate CKD. [PMID-16194413] The same abstract evaluated ACE inhibitor use in patients with serum creatinine above 266 micromol/L. [PMID-16194413] *Patient X is already on lisinopril, so the question is not whether an ACE inhibitor has potential renal value but whether BP control and safety monitoring are adequate. [SQL:medication] [SQL:vital_sign]*\n- The PubMed searches performed in this run did not return citable, directly relevant trial abstracts for SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 receptor agonist renal outcome benefits in this patient\u2019s exact scenario, nor direct abstracts on sulfonylurea hypoglycemia in CKD or NSAID plus ACE-inhibitor CKD risk. [PMID-22545920] *Medication alternatives and NSAID risk should still be reviewed clinically, but this brief cites only the evidence IDs returned in this run. [PMID-22545920]*\n\n## Questions for the visit\n1. Has Patient X had any repeat creatinine/eGFR, potassium, or UACR since 2026-08-26, and should these be ordered today?\n2. Can the patient stop or reduce ibuprofen and use safer knee-pain options while kidney function is declining?\n3. Should glipizide be reduced, stopped, or replaced given symptomatic low glucose with skipped meals despite HbA1c 8.6%?\n4. Is metformin 1000 mg twice daily still appropriate at eGFR 47 with a steep downward eGFR slope, and what sick-day instructions are needed?\n5. What BP goal is appropriate, and should lisinopril be titrated or another antihypertensive added given BP 149/90 and albuminuria?\n6. Should additional kidney- and cardiovascular-protective diabetes therapy be considered, using local guidelines and formulary criteria?\n7. Can retinal screening be rescheduled before the patient leaves, and is a foot exam due today?\n\n## Sources\n- [SQL:patient] Patient demographics and follow-up context\n- [SQL:condition] Diagnoses and condition history\n- [SQL:lab_result] Laboratory results including HbA1c, eGFR, and UACR\n- [SQL:cohort_benchmark] Type 2 diabetes cohort benchmark percentiles and slopes\n- [SQL:vital_sign] Vital signs including blood pressure\n- [SQL:medication] Active medication list with doses and dates\n- [SQL:referral] Referral records including ophthalmology diabetic retinal screening\n- [SYN-X-NOTE-0001] Clinical note from 2025-03-04 primary care visit\n- [SYN-X-NOTE-0002] Clinical note from 2025-06-12 ophthalmology referral status\n- [SYN-X-NOTE-0003] Clinical note from 2025-09-10 primary care visit\n- [SYN-X-NOTE-0004] Clinical note from 2026-01-22 nurse telephone call\n- [SYN-X-NOTE-0005] Clinical note from 2026-03-18 primary care visit\n- [SYN-X-NOTE-0006] Clinical note from 2026-08-26 lab result note\n- [SYN-X-NOTE-0007] Clinical note from 2026-09-02 care coordinator message\n- [MEDQUAD-02122#chunk-1] How to diagnose Diabetic Kidney Disease ?\n- [MEDQUAD-02123#chunk-0] How to prevent Diabetic Kidney Disease ?\n- [MEDQUAD-02123#chunk-2] How to prevent Diabetic Kidney Disease ?\n- [MEDQUAD-01393#chunk-0] How to prevent Hypoglycemia ?\n- [MEDQUAD-01393#chunk-1] How to prevent Hypoglycemia ?\n- [PMID-22545920] Does stage III chronic kidney disease always progress to end-stage renal disease?\n- [PMID-25746373] Is albuminuria a myocardial infarction risk equivalent for atherothrombotic events?\n- [PMID-24636277] Is chronic kidney disease associated with diabetic retinopathy in Asian adults?\n- [PMID-16194413] Can angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor be used in chronic kidney disease patients with serum creatinine level greater than 266 micromol/L?\n"}
